{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Our hands did not shed.</b> It is written with a heh to teach that the host is obligated to do five things for a guest: food, drink, lodging, escort, provisions for the road, and also a gift. And in tractate Sotah, chapter Eglah Arufah, it concludes: ״ידינו לא שפכה״ — would it enter your mind that the court are shedders of blood? Rather: he did not come into our hands and we dismissed him without provisions; he did not come into our hands and we dismissed him without escort — for this is what it implies. But had we seen him, we would have escorted him, and he would not have been harmed, for Rav Yehudah said in the name of Rav: anyone who escorts his fellow even four cubits in the city is not harmed. Ravina would escort Rav Nakhman bar Yitzhak — he said: I bring myself into harm's way, and I escape. And the Rabbis taught: the teacher [escorts] the student until the boundary of the city; a fellow [escorts] a fellow until the Shabbat limit; a student [escorts] his teacher has no measure — and how much? Rav Sheshet said: a parasang; an outstanding teacher: three parasangs. And it was taught: Rabbi Meir says: they compel for escort, and escort has no measure, as it is said: ״ויראו איש יוצא מן העיר ויאמרו אליו הראנו את מבוא העיר ועשינו עמך חסד״, and it is written: ״ויראם את מבוא העיר״. And what kindness did they do with him? That they killed the whole city by the sword, but that man and his family they saved, as it is written: ״וילך האיש ארץ החתים ויבן העיר ויקרא שמה לוז״. And when Sennacherib came he did not disturb it, and Nebuchadnezzar did not destroy it, and even the angel of death has no permission to stand in it; rather, the elders among them whose spirits are weary of their lives take them outside the wall and they die. This is an a fortiori: if this Canaanite, who did not speak with his mouth and did not go with his feet, brought salvation to himself and to his seed until the end of all generations — one who escorts with his mouth and with his feet, all the more so. And Rabbi Yehoshua said: on account of four cubits that Egypt escorted Abraham our father, they were enslaved in his children four hundred years. And some say that escort is a measure of one mil, and a support for the matter: ״כי מלאכיו יצוה לך לשמרך בכל דרכיך״ etc. — the mem of מלאכיו, the yod of יצוה, the lamed of לך — behold, a mil. And then he is assured to be protected in all his ways. And when the guest goes out on the road, even though the host gives him coins, he must give him bread, as it is said: ״ועל פת לחם יפשע גבר״ — for perhaps a bulmus may seize him on the road and he will die, and the host is found to have transgressed for having let him leave without bread. And Rabbi Yochanan said: anyone who does not escort is as if he sheds blood, for had the people of Jericho escorted Elisha, he would not have decreed bears upon the children, as it is said: ״ויעל משם בית אל והוא עולה בדרך ונערים קטנים יוצאים ויתקלסו בו ויאמרו עלה קרח עלה קרח״ — they said: make the place bald upon us. What are 'young children'? Rabbi Elazar said: people who are shaken free of commandments; 'small' — that they were belittlers of faith. And it is written: ״ויפן אחריו ויראם ויקללם בשם ה'״ — what did he see? That from all of them their mother conceived on Yom Kippur. And Rabbi Yochanan said: he saw that there was in them no moisture of commandments, neither in them nor in their seed. And furthermore, escort is greater than a gift, for a gift is with one's money, but escort is with one's body. And furthermore, both the one who escorts and the one who is escorted are not harmed, as it is said: ״שלום שלום לרחוק״ — that is the one who distances himself from the city, that is the one who is escorted; ״ולקרוב״ — the one who draws near to the city, that is the one who escorts, who returns to the city. And furthermore, it is in the Yerushalmi: ״אפריון נמטייה למאן דמלוה״ — meaning: favor reaches those who escort those who depart on the road, for Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: one who walks on the road and has no escort should engage in Torah, as it is said: ״כי לוית חן הם לראשך״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The heh referenced at the opening likely refers to the word לא being written with a full spelling or a variant tradition; the exact orthographic point is uncertain from the text as given. The word נמטייה is Aramaic for 'those who depart/travel.' The phrase קטני אמנה likely means 'belittlers of faith' but could also be read as 'of little faith.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:25.085490588Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}